Subject: [PATCH 4/6] phyFLEX-stm32mp2 M33 SPI loopback example: stm32cubemp2
 v1.3.2

   The default SPI_FullDuplex_ComDMA example has the following behavior:

   - uses SPI3 to communicate between two STM32MP257-EV1 boards using DMA.
   - it requires configuring one board as master and the other as slave.
   - waits for a USER2 button press before the master exchanges a fixed message.
   - performs the exchange once and compares the transmit and receive buffers.
   - blinks LED3 on success.

   Here we are changing the example to run SPI2 as master on one
   phyFLEX-STM32MP257 Development Kit, with MOSI connected back to MISO
   using a jumper wire for a hardware loopback test:

   - uses PB0 for SPI2 SCK, PB2 for MOSI, and PB6 for MISO.
   - connect PB2 to PB6 for external loopback.
   - uses SPI mode 0 with 8-bit data, MSB first.
   - starts one DMA test for each debounced USER2 button press.
   - transmits DE AD BE EF CA FE and receives into a separate buffer.
   - prints TX/RX data and PASS/FAIL through UART5 at 115200 baud, 8N1,
     without flow control.
   - leaves LED3 on after failure and clears it after a successful test.
   - handles transfer timeouts and allows subsequent tests to retry.

   Connecting/removing the loopback wire can induce a successful
   test, as well as a data-mismatch failure in order to verify the
   interface.

   In production boot mode, SPI2 needs a clock source managed by OP-TEE
   and this is not enabled by default in the current phyFLEX-STM32MP257 BSP.
   The SPI_FullDuplex_ComDMA example now uses ST's existing SCMI middleware
   to request OP-TEE for that clock when the firmware starts.

   The STM32MP257 supplies SPI2's kernel clock from FLEXGEN channel 10
   (ck_flexgen_10). The M33 firmware requests this clock from OP-TEE
   using SCMI clock ID 10.

Signed-off-by: True Loan <tloan@phytec.com>
